Project Summary: Core Service (Restaurant Management System)
Architecture Overview
- Stack: Node.js/Express + TypeScript + PostgreSQL (Knex) + Redis + JWT
- Pattern: Clean Architecture — Controller → Service → Repository → Database
- DI: tsyringe with singleton-scoped services
- Validation: class-validator + class-transformer via validateBody()
- Auth: JWT in httpOnly cookies (access 1h, refresh longer), bcrypt passwords
Domain Modules & Workflows
Module	Purpose	Key Flows
Auth	Login/Register/Forgot/Reset/Invite	Register → (if restaurant_user) create Restaurant + Owner Member → issue tokens
User	Profile, password update	Standard CRUD + password hashing
Restaurant	Restaurant entity + owner linkage	Admin creates with owner; owner updates own; admin changes status
Branch	Physical locations per restaurant	Owner/admin CRUD; geo search (findNearBranches)
Product	Menu items with branch-level price/stock	Owner/admin CRUD; auto-get-or-create category; branch details (PBD)
RBAC (Member)	Team management per restaurant	Invite (inactive + OTP email) → accept invite (set password + activate) → assign role + branches
Customer Address	Delivery addresses for customers	Standard CRUD with default flag
RBAC / Permission Model
System Roles: system_admin | restaurant_user | customer | delivery_agent

Restaurant Roles (for restaurant_user):
  - owner: ALL permissions
  - branch_manager: product CRUD, member read, branch update
  - staff: product read, member read

Permissions (resource:action):
  core:product:create/read/update
  core:member:create/read/update/delete
  core:branch:create/update
  core:restaurant:update
- Middleware: rbac({resource, action}) checks PermissionCacheService (1h TTL, in-memory Map)
- Ownership guards: requireRestaurantMember, requireBranchAccess
Key Strengths
1. Layer separation is clean — controllers are thin, services hold business logic, repositories do SQL.
2. Transactions used correctly for multi-table writes (register, create member, create restaurant).
3. DI tokens are centralized; easy to swap implementations.
4. Migrations are explicit, idempotent, with indexes on FKs.
5. Correlation IDs on every request for tracing.
6. Error handling via AppError + global handler — operational vs. unexpected errors distinguished.
Issues & Refactoring Hints
1. Critical: requireBranchAccess Bug (line 84 in rbac.ts)
// CURRENT — compares entire array to single number
if (Number(req.user.branchIds) == Number(branchId))  // ❌ branchIds is number[]

// FIX — check inclusion
if (req.user.branchIds?.includes(branchId))
This middleware will never allow access for branch-scoped routes.
2. Permission Cache Not Invalidated
- PermissionCacheService uses in-memory Map with 1h TTL.
- No invalidation on role/permission changes (e.g., after updateMember, deleteMember, or direct DB changes).
- Fix: Add invalidate(roleName) and call it in MemberService.update/delete, or switch to Redis with pub/sub invalidation.
3. acceptInvite Uses Wrong DTO
// auth.controller.ts:74
const data = await validateBody(ResetPasswordDTO, req.body);
Should use a dedicated AcceptInviteDTO (needs email, password, otp — not just newPassword).
4. N+1 Risk in findByIdWithRestaurant (BranchService)
const branch = await this.branchRepository.findById(branchId);
const restaurant = await this.restaurantService.getById(branch.restaurantId); // 2nd query
Use a JOIN or findByIdWithRestaurant in repository to fetch in one query.
5. Inconsistent Return Shapes
Some services return { message, data }, others return raw entities, others return { message } only.
- Standardize: Always { message: string, data?: T } for success.
6. Missing Authorization on Some Routes
- GET /branch/:id (findByIdWithRestaurant) — no rbac or requireBranchAccess middleware on route.
- GET /product/branch/:branchId — no check that user belongs to that branch/restaurant.
- Audit all routes and attach appropriate middleware.
7. refresh Token Rotation Not Implemented
- Refresh endpoint issues new access token but does not rotate refresh token.
- If refresh token is stolen, attacker has persistent access.
- Fix: Issue new refresh token + invalidate old one (store token hash or JTI in DB).
8. createPasswordReset Leaks User Existence
// auth.service.ts:190-194
const user = await this.userService.getByEmail(email);
if (!user) return; // silently returns — no OTP sent
This allows email enumeration. Always return success even if email not found (already done in forgetPassword but the helper exposes the leak).
9. Hardcoded Commission/Status Defaults
// branch.service.ts:98-101
isActive: false,
acceptOrders: true,
currency: data.currency,
commission: 0,  // hardcoded
Move to config or restaurant-level defaults.
10. No Pagination on List Endpoints
- listMembers, getByRestaurant, findByRestaurant, findCategories — return all rows.
- Add limit/offset or cursor pagination.
11. Type Safety: req.user Augmentation
- express.d.ts should define Request.user with exact shape:
interface UserPayload {
  userId: number;
  email: string;
  role: SystemRole;
  restaurantId?: number;
  restaurantRole?: string;
  branchIds?: number[];
}
Currently req.user is any — loses IntelliSense.
12. Migration: restaurants Table Missing ownerId FK Index
Check migration 20261009230442_create_restaurants_table.ts — ensure ownerId has index (used in RestaurantService.update ownership check).
13. Testing Gap
- No test files visible. Add:
- Unit tests for services (mock repositories)
- Integration tests for auth/RBAC flows
- Contract tests for DTOs
14. Observability
- Logger only logs errors. Add request logging (method, path, status, latency) via middleware.
- Health check only pings DB — add Redis, disk space, memory.
